      "title": "Florida sues Stake and VGW and names Worldpay, Trustly, Praxis and Breeze Labs as defendants",
      "text": "Florida Attorney General James Uthmeier filed two lawsuits on Wednesday in Hillsborough County Circuit Court against sweepstakes casino operators Stake and VGW, whose brands include Chumba Casino, LuckyLand and Global Poker, seeking permanent injunctions to stop them operating in the state. What makes this filing different from the dozen-plus state actions that preceded it is that the suits also name four payment companies as defendants: Worldpay, Trustly, Praxis and Breeze Labs. Uthmeier said: \"If it looks like a casino, takes money like a casino, and pays out like a casino, it is a casino - and it's illegal in Florida. That's why we're suing predatory online gaming sites Stake and VGW to stop illegal gambling and recover money lost by Florida consumers.\" He added that the companies \"market themselves as sweepstakes or social casinos, but they offer real gambling - slots, blackjack, and poker,\" and had been \"preying on Floridians, including minors and seniors, with deceptive marketing and 24/7 access, while dodging our state's licensing, taxation, and consumer-protection requirements.\" The claims seek disgorgement, civil penalties under Florida's Deceptive and Unfair Trade Practices Act and attorneys' fees. Pulling processors into the defendant list moves sweepstakes exposure from a legal-risk question into a PSP risk-committee question, and that is where cashier availability for adjacent regulated products gets decided.",
