      "title": "Player data as a regulated asset in Africa",
      "text": "Uganda's National Lotteries and Gaming Regulatory Board ran a compliance engagement for licensed operators built on three pillars - regulatory compliance, anti-money laundering obligations and responsible gaming - but the detail that matters for platform design is who else was in the room. The Personal Data Protection Office presented on lawful processing, protection of customer information, data-subject rights and accountability, with officials saying that being licensed and financially transparent is \"no longer enough\" and that operators must also protect, store and responsibly use player data including identity-verification details and transaction histories. That is a meaningful shift for African market entry, where KYC document handling has often been treated as an operational detail. Retention policies, subject-access response paths and encryption of verification artefacts become licensing-adjacent requirements, and they are considerably harder to retrofit than to build in at launch.",
