    "title": "Romania's ONJN builds real-time transaction monitoring and digital self-exclusion after the EUR900m tax scandal",
    "meta": "SBC News | 19 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Romania's Court of Auditors reports significant progress at gambling regulator ONJN, which has audited five fiscal years of land-based declarations, opened probes into suspected GGR manipulation and built a real-time metadata analysis system with the country's Digital Authority.",
    "why": "A regulator running real-time metadata analysis against operator transactions changes what reporting means in Romania: the platform has to produce a continuous, reconcilable stream rather than periodic returns, and the proposed self-exclusion ordinance would add a cross-operator exclusion integration on top of it.",
    "expanded": "Written by Viktor Kayed. The Court of Auditors' 2025 report follows the early-2025 scandal in which more than EUR900m in missing taxes was found to be absent from ONJN's records, prompting the then-president's resignation. Vlad-Cristian Soare took over in May 2025. Since then ONJN has investigated land-based operator declarations going back five fiscal years and started fund-recoupment procedures, and opened probes into suspected GGR manipulation by online operators. It worked with Romania's Digital Authority to build a real-time metadata analysis system for detecting suspicious transactions. The Responsible Gambling Promotion Service, previously found to be collecting funds without running any problem-gambling campaigns, now has a state programme with first grant recipients due to receive EUR5m in total. Soare also launched a fully digital self-exclusion tool, though current legislation limits its rollout across online operators and an Emergency Ordinance has been proposed to fast-track it. Soare said: \"We are not just talking about administrative responses to some findings, but about concrete changes: controls on the last five fiscal years, recovery of differences due, monitoring of online operators, combating the black market, financing addiction prevention and treatment programs and transforming self-exclusion into a functional and digitalized system. There are still things to be done, but the changes are visible, the problems are identified, the measures are underway, and the results can be verified.\"",
