    "meta": "SBC News | 18 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "The Korea Communications Standards Commission moved to block access to Polymarket after a month-long dispute, rejecting the platform's argument that peer-to-peer blockchain settlement exempts it from South Korean gambling and financial regulation.",
    "why": "The KCSC's reasoning, that targeting local subject matter creates a practical gambling environment regardless of settlement architecture, is the test other Asian regulators are likely to copy, and it applies to any product localised by content rather than by licence.",
    "expanded": "The dispute began in early July over illegal gambling allegations. Polymarket argued its blockchain-based contracts should exempt it from South Korean financial regulation, noting it had removed its Korean-language service and does not support Korean Won payments, and stating: \"Because the platform operates via non-custodial peer-to-peer (P2P) transactions and smart contracts, we do not act as an 'organiser',\" and \"Furthermore, because we do not directly collect or manage funds, nor do we issue sports lottery tickets, we do not satisfy the legal requirements for violating the Criminal Act or the National Sports Promotion Act, nor do we meet the criteria for speculative/gambling activities.\" The KCSC rejected this, responding: \"The platform cannot evade the application of domestic laws simply by citing technical characteristics or service delivery methods... Because Polymarket targets South Korea-specific issues (such as 'August Precipitation in Seoul') and provides a practical illegal gambling environment to domestic users based on a winner-take-all profit/loss structure driven by chance, an access block is inevitable to protect domestic users.\" Indonesia, Singapore and Myanmar have also restricted Polymarket in Asia.",
