    "title": "GCI estimates $409bn of World Cup betting handle went through unregulated platforms reached via illegal streams",
    "meta": "Covers | 19 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Gaming Compliance International estimated the 2026 World Cup generated $593bn in global online betting handle, of which $409bn, or 69%, went through unregulated platforms, and found 95% of qualifying illegal stream views carried unregulated sportsbook advertising.",
    "why": "This reframes piracy as an acquisition channel competing directly with licensed operators on the same event inventory, which puts data-rights and stream-integrity work alongside affiliate compliance rather than in a separate content-protection silo.",
    "expanded": "Reported by Grant Mitchell. GCI estimated illegal streams generated 174.3 billion qualifying views globally during the tournament, each at least 90 seconds long, with $184bn, or 31%, of handle going through regulated platforms. Illegal streaming platforms can receive 25% to 50% of net gaming revenue from referred customers via affiliate deals; GCI calculated that if just one in 1,000 qualifying views produced a referred customer generating $10 in net gaming revenue, a streamer could earn roughly $435.8m via a 25% referral deal. GCI CEO Matt Holt said: \"When 95% of qualifying illegal stream views carry advertising for unregulated gambling, illegal streaming is not simply stealing content. It is providing one of the world's largest sporting audiences as an acquisition channel for the unregulated gambling economy.\" GCI president Ismail Vali said: \"The World Cup showed us the dark nexus between illegal streaming and unregulated gambling at a scale we have never measured before... Illegal streaming steals the same content, puts it all in one place for 'free,' and monetizes the audience through unregulated gambling and other forms of crime.\" GCI called for targeting the broader illegal ecosystem rather than individual URLs.",
