    "title": "1xCare advisory member Sissel Weitzhandler sets out a funding standard built on measurable outcomes",
    "meta": "Yogonet | 19 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Sissel Weitzhandler, a member of 1xCare's advisory committee, described how the operator-funded body tries to establish independence and accountability, saying it has committed to regular impact reporting with a first report planned for 2027.",
    "why": "Operator-funded harm-reduction bodies are becoming a stated licensing expectation in several markets, and the measurement discipline they adopt tends to become the evidence standard operators are later asked to meet on their own RG interventions.",
    "expanded": "The interview, originally published by iGaming News, covers Weitzhandler's background in International Relations and War Studies and prior experience at Allwyn across licensing, governance, AML, market-entry strategy and player protection. 1xCare is funded by the owner of the 1xBet brand but governed independently via its advisory committee. She said: \"Gambling brought those questions into sharp focus... Good regulation has to be practical enough to work in a real commercial and product environment, while being robust enough to protect the public interest.\" On independence she said she checked \"where do funding decisions sit, can the committee challenge or redirect a proposal, and how will uncomfortable findings be handled.\" On accountability: \"A credible standard starts before any money moves. We agree what the grant is for, what success would look like, and how it will be measured... I would rather fund an organisation that tells me plainly where it struggled than one that reports only good news.\" On evidence: \"Where evidence is thin, the answer should not be to fill the gap with confidence. It should be to fund cautiously, define the learning objective.\" On diversity: \"Diversity is not a guarantee of the right answer. It is a safeguard against the wrong answer going insufficiently examined.\"",
