    "title": "Daniel Wallach maps a Supreme Court path for sports event contracts as leagues stay in wait-and-see mode",
    "meta": "Covers | 18 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Gaming attorney Daniel Wallach and American University gaming professor Matthew Bakowicz explain why only three major US teams have signed prediction market deals, pointing to regulatory uncertainty and integrity protections absent from the Commodity Exchange Act.",
    "why": "The legal timetable rather than the product timetable determines whether exchange-style event contracts become a permanent parallel channel or a contained experiment, and that timetable now runs to 2027 at the earliest.",
    "expanded": "Only a handful of major US sports teams have partnered with prediction market operators: the New York Mets signed with Novig in late July 2026, the first MLB team to do so; the New York Yankees made Polymarket their official prediction market operator in early August 2026; and the Chicago Blackhawks partnered with Kalshi in late 2025. MLB signed a memorandum of understanding with the CFTC, with CFTC chair Michael Selig calling it \"a collaborative step towards promoting integrity and resilience of the prediction markets relating to professional baseball.\" More than $173bn has been traded on Kalshi since its autumn 2024 launch. Mets owner Steve Cohen is separately building an $8bn casino project with Hard Rock next to Citi Field after winning one of three downstate New York licences in December 2025. Reported by Brad Senkiw.",
    "commentary": "Wallach's read is that the NFL and NBA are \"somewhat in a wait-and-see posture,\" partly because integrity protections that exist in state sports betting law are absent from the Commodity Exchange Act and CFTC regulations. He expects the question of whether sports event contracts are bets or swaps to reach the Supreme Court, potentially via a New Jersey case that could arrive \"late next year,\" with a ruling possible \"by the last week of June of 2027\" in the fastest scenario; otherwise cases in Nevada, Tennessee or Ohio could push resolution to 2028. Bakowicz frames team reluctance commercially rather than legally: \"I think it's fear of the fact that there's uncertainty in how the market is going to be regulated... for many of these owners and for many of these teams, they have business interests outside of their own state and internationally and worldwide.\" He described Cohen's position as investing in sports betting with Hard Rock and in prediction markets at the same time.",
    "takeaway": "A federal resolution in mid-2027 at the earliest means the sportsbook-versus-exchange question stays open through two more product cycles, so the pragmatic platform position is to keep event-contract capability optional and separable rather than committing to either architecture on the assumption the law settles soon.",
